+# 🌉 Bridge Work Order — [P1] Memory-aware chat UX missing: 20-tool memory layer and a living knowledge graph (35 entities / 71 relations) are invisible in chat (Monica Gap 5/8) (bridge-20261027-audit-gap-memory-chat-ux)
+
+## Problem
+The Bangla feature audit (2026-10-27) mapped Monica's memory-aware chat experience as Gap 5. SupremeAI's situation is the sharpest shell/engine inversion in the audit: the memory backend is richer than the benchmark's, and the user-facing product shows none of it.
+
+Verified live the same day:
+- `memory_status`: ready, 20 memory tools, no errors.
+- The knowledge graph seeded earlier the same day (15 entities / 16 relations, fixing the then-empty graph #1341) has **self-grown to 35 entities / 71 relations** — the memory subsystem is not just alive, it is compounding.
+- Episodes/session machinery active across clients.
+
+Yet Studio chat exposes zero memory affordances. Nothing tells a user "I remember your stack from Tuesday"; nothing lets them see what will be recalled, correct it, or forget it. The exact cross-session recall that makes a competitor's product feel personal (AIQ-057/098/099 scored partial for precisely this reason) is running dark, one API call away from the UI.
+
+## Impact
+- Personalness: memory is the #1 "this product knows me" signal; without surfaced memory, every chat restarts from zero emotionally even when the backend recalls.
+- Trust and control: users who suspect memory but cannot see or erase it assume the worst (privacy anxiety); visible chips + forget buttons convert anxiety into control.
+- Wasted compounding asset: the graph grows automatically; nobody harvests the value.
+
+## Fix (smallest safe change)
+Read-only UI over the existing memory tools — no new storage, no new backend subsystem.
+1. Remembered-context chips: before each send, run the existing memory search for the active session namespace and render the top recalled entities/relations as chips above the composer ("Recalling: tenant staging-admin · streaming r6 · redis rotation").
+2. Continuity indicator: one-line label in the chat header — "N facts recalled from M past sessions" — honest zero when nothing recalled (never fabricate memory).
+3. Per-chip forget: X on each chip calls the existing memory deletion tool for that node; the next turn no longer recalls it.
+4. Namespacing discipline: chips read and forget strictly within the current tenant:user:session namespace (2 active tenants verified isolated — the negative battery from the tenant runbook applies here too).
+
+## Acceptance Criteria
+- [ ] Chips show real recalled nodes for the active namespace (verified against direct memory tool output — not placeholder data)
+- [ ] Forget removes the node; the same query next turn returns without it
+- [ ] Zero-recall state renders the honest "no memory recalled" state — no fabricated chips
+- [ ] Cross-tenant negative test: tenant A never sees tenant B's chips or history
+- [ ] No regression in message send latency beyond one parallel memory-search call (async, non-blocking)
+
+## Reflection Evidence (why + alternatives_rejected)
+**Why:** the backend already outbuilt the shell here — this is the highest gap-closing ratio per hour in the whole audit (estimated ~2 hours to first visible value); memory made visible also feeds the eval loop (recall correctness becomes checkable).
+**Alternatives rejected:** (a) a full memory-manager settings page — valuable but deferred; chips prove the value first with minimal surface; (b) exposing raw graph JSON — developer debugging, not user experience; (c) auto-summarizing "what we know about you" essays — high hallucination risk, chips stay atomic and verifiable.
+
+## Labels
+`bridge:queued` `P1-high` `area:studio` `area:memory` `gap:monica-05`
